WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.036
Start here: how the exam works and how to use this track.

2
00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:15.327
The exam tests judgment on realistic systems, not trivia, so the fastest way to pass is to study the guide's task statements, build a little, and practise picking the root-cause fix.

3
00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:17.234
Welcome.

4
00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:22.054
This track prepares you for the Claude Certified Architect Foundations exam.

5
00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:25.162
It has sixty questions in two hours, closed book.

6
00:00:25.162 --> 00:00:30.869
Scores run from one hundred to one thousand and the pass line is seven hundred and twenty.

7
00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:37.719
Each question is framed by a realistic scenario, and you get four of the six published scenarios on the day.

8
00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:42.581
The exam guide splits the material into five domains.

9
00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:47.285
Agentic architecture is the biggest at about a quarter of the questions.

10
00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:50.683
Claude Code and prompt engineering are a fifth each.

11
00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:57.085
Tool design and MCP is just under a fifth, and context management and reliability is the smallest.

12
00:00:57.085 --> 00:01:00.025
Spend your time in roughly those proportions.

13
00:01:01.425 --> 00:01:03.178
The track has three stages.

14
00:01:03.178 --> 00:01:08.500
Stage one gives you a short primer for each domain, so the vocabulary is familiar.

15
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.589
Stage two has one lesson for every task statement in the guide.

16
00:01:12.589 --> 00:01:16.938
Stage three walks through the six scenarios and ends with exam day.

17
00:01:16.938 --> 00:01:22.844
Every lesson has slides, a narrated video, a small hands-on lab, a quick check and a drill.

18
00:01:24.244 --> 00:01:25.793
Here is a loop that works.

19
00:01:25.793 --> 00:01:29.487
Start with the placement check so you know where you are weak.

20
00:01:29.487 --> 00:01:34.908
For each weak task statement, take the lesson and do the lab, because hands-on time sticks.

21
00:01:34.908 --> 00:01:35.742
Then drill it.

22
00:01:35.742 --> 00:01:39.614
Every miss lands in the Mistake Clinic, so clear those regularly.

23
00:01:39.614 --> 00:01:43.487
Once a week, sit a full mock exam and watch the domain bars move.

24
00:01:44.887 --> 00:01:47.716
Most questions have several plausible answers.

25
00:01:47.716 --> 00:01:48.823
Three habits help.

26
00:01:48.823 --> 00:01:57.556
Prefer the option that fixes the root cause over one that patches the symptom, especially a stronger prompt where code could enforce the rule.

27
00:01:57.556 --> 00:02:00.447
Prefer the simplest design that meets the need.

28
00:02:00.447 --> 00:02:04.321
And use the out-of-scope list in the guide to rule options out.

29
00:02:05.721 --> 00:02:06.346
To sum up.

30
00:02:06.346 --> 00:02:14.532
The official exam guide is the authority, and this track is unofficial study help built from it and from Anthropic's documentation.

31
00:02:14.532 --> 00:02:19.282
Study task statement by task statement, in proportion to the domain weights.

32
00:02:19.282 --> 00:02:21.219
Build a little in every domain.

33
00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:26.031
And on the day, choose the root-cause fix and the simplest design that works.

